Real Market Example

Suppose:

Reliance Share

moves like this for several days:
| Day       | Price |
| --------- | ----- |
| Monday    | ₹1480 |
| Tuesday   | ₹1500 |
| Wednesday | ₹1490 |
| Thursday  | ₹1500 |
| Friday    | ₹1485 |


You notice:

₹1500

is difficult to cross.

Therefore:

₹1500 = Resistance

Similarly:

| Day       | Price |
| --------- | ----- |
| Monday    | ₹1400 |
| Tuesday   | ₹1405 |
| Wednesday | ₹1400 |
| Thursday  | ₹1402 |
| Friday    | ₹1400 |

Then:

₹1400 = Support

What are Time Frames?

A time frame tells how much time one candle represents.

1 Minute Chart
One candle = 1 minute

Example:

9:15 AM → Candle 1
9:16 AM → Candle 2
9:17 AM → Candle 3

Used by:

Scalpers
Very short-term traders

========

5 Minute Chart
One candle = 5 minutes

Example:

9:15–9:20 = One candle
9:20–9:25 = One candle

Used by:

Intraday traders
15 Minute Chart
One candle = 15 minutes

Example:

9:15–9:30 = One candle
9:30–9:45 = One candle

Used by:

Intraday traders
Beginners
1 Hour Chart
One candle = 1 hour

Example:

9:15–10:15 = One candle
10:15–11:15 = One candle

Used by:

Swing traders
1 Day Chart
One candle = 1 trading day

Example:

Monday = One candle
Tuesday = One candle
Wednesday = One candle

Used by:

Investors
Swing traders
1 Week Chart
One candle = 1 week

Example:

Week 1 = One candle
Week 2 = One candle

Used by:

Long-term investors
1 Month Chart
One candle = 1 month

Example:

January = One candle
February = One candle
March = One candle

Used by:

Long-term investors
Portfolio managers

